From b4c4bd7a9834465fb65a578bf9a419f38b116283 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joachim Wiberg <troglobit@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 11:20:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 149/156] net: sparx5: apply the priority map of the root qdisc
 in the fabric
Organization: Wires

An ets qdisc is offloaded only with the plain reversed priomap, so a
root qdisc that queues priority 1 below priority 0, as IEEE 802.1Q
Table 8-5 does, is refused:

    tc qdisc add dev eth0 root ets bands 8 strict 8 priomap 6 7 5 4 3 2 1 0

An mqprio or taprio map is taken, yet holds for the CPU path only.
Frames forwarded by the fabric are queued by the priority their ingress
port classified, whatever the map says.

The queue system looks up the scheduler element of a frame per egress
port, in XQS:QMAP_SE_TBL at the port's virtual port plus the classified
QoS class.  At reset every class leads to the element of its own number.

Write the map of the root qdisc there, so a frame is queued as the port
it leaves by says, and keeps its priority for the PCP and DSCP rewrite.
Frames the CPU receives get their priority from the classified QoS class
in the extraction header, and frames it sends carry their priority in
the injection header, as lan966x and the Ocelot tagger do.

Signed-off-by: Joachim Wiberg <troglobit@gmail.com>
